the town of lytton near the west coast  in british columbia  endured temperatures close to 50 degrees  on tuesday  the day before more than a thousand  residents from the area were forced to  flee their homes because of the flames  gordon murray was one of those who had  to evacuate and captured this  extraordinary footage and gordon joins  me now  gordon thank you for being with us you  have  captured scenes of absolute devastation  just describe for us what it was like  well thanks for having me it was uh a  puck  apocalyptic is really the only words i  can use to describe it  without exaggeration as you can see from  the footage there were fires  on both sides of the road uh and their  buildings just bursting into flames  trees exploding you could hear them  uh it was and we had no really no  idea how bad it was and uh because there  was no warnings  from uh from anyone of what was  happening we just had  we basically had to figure out what was  going on  and uh when the cell service died we had  been trying to figure out you know  trying to check  uh cell and uh internet to find out what  what the situation was and there was no  information so  when the cell service died we realized  we just had to get out of there so  we grabbed our pets and just started  driving we had no we didn't even know  which way to head  because there was smoke and fire  everywhere and fortunately  we made it through and and where are you  and how are you now gordon  uh we're now i mean we're in shock  obviously it was  it's hard to believe there was only like  36 hours ago it seems like 36 days  um but we're staying with family in  vancouver  so we're relatively uh you know that  that's  a long way away from where we were um  but it's it's it's difficult we've we've  just discovered  basically last night because we when we  left our house was still intact but we  discovered that the  there's been some aerial photos  published in our house and 95  if not 99 of the other houses have been  completely  wiped off the map the town doesn't exist  anymore  oh i'm i'm so sorry to hear that  uh gordon what was going through  your mind as you were driving through  those flames given that the speed  that this had engulfed the the community  catching residents by surprise and  you're driving trying to get out of it  well yeah i was just complete shocked  because we had no idea  that it was this bad as i said we were  on the edge of the boat we were  on the uh the edge of the village away  from where the fire started so we didn't  realize how bad it was until we  started driving through it um but i just  want to  point out that this is really you know  it's a litten story but it goes far  beyond that because that that day before  four other communities in bc exceeded  the all-time canadian uh  heat record so this is a climate change  story it's just there's no doubt about  it there's  the the uh you know we  we were not prepared for it and uh  you know london is at about the same  latitude as litton  so this is coming for for everybody even  in temperate  countries don't uh we we need to uh  start we're long past starting we've  been we should have started a long time  ago but we need to tackle climate change  in a very very serious way  we need to decarbonize our economy uh  because this is going to be coming for  all  everyone very soon that that's that's  the story here is that  nobody thought it was going to happen  nobody knew it was going to happen and  with 15 minutes  15 minutes the entire town was on fire  um gordon we are so very sorry uh for  you thank you  for talking to us our thoughts are with  you and the very best of luck for the  weeks and months ahead
 

